<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Geumgwan Gaya</h1><p class="st-meta">42 CE – 532 CE (approx.) · capital Gimhae</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading early state of the Gaya confederacy at modern Gimhae, a port kingdom whose iron trade linked Korea, Chinese commanderies and Japan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Geumgwan Gaya grew out of Guya-guk, a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byeonhan">Byeonhan</a> statelet at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakdong-river">Nakdong</a> river. Its founding legend tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suro-of-geumgwan-gaya">King Suro</a> hatching from a golden egg in 42 CE and marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heo-hwang-ok">Heo Hwang-ok</a>, a princess said to have sailed from a distant land called Ayuta.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a> controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">iron production</a> and a harbour where traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery">Lelang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daifang-commandery">Daifang</a> and Wa called. The royal cemetery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daeseong-dong-tumuli">Daeseong-dong</a> contains iron armour, horse gear and weapons, plus Japanese-style objects. Until about 400 it led the Gaya states, but a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> campaign that year to aid <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a> broke its strength.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened and squeezed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guhyeong-of-geumgwan-gaya">Guhyeong</a> surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beopheung-of-silla">King Beopheung</a> of Silla in 532 and was given rank and land. His great-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yu-sin</a> became the general who helped Silla unify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 42 CE – 532 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Korea and Japan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:60.54%;width:9.75%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>42 CE – 532 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gimhae</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Suro of Geumgwan Gaya, Guhyeong of Geumgwan Gaya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaya language</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bon Gaya, Garakguk, Kumgwan Kaya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=287&amp;focus=geumgwan-gaya" aria-label="Geumgwan Gaya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7943 904 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Geumgwan Gaya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8263 1160L8247 1162M8263 1160L8260 1139"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8247 1162h0M8260 1139h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8263 1160h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8263 1160h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gimhae. <a href="/atlas?year=287&amp;focus=geumgwan-gaya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
